roject.toml", "scripts/test.sh", "tests/clock_isolation.py", "tests/enforcement/_function_level_import_exemptions.txt", "tests/enforcement/_model_literal_exemptions.txt", "tests/enforcement/_public_api_consumers.txt", "tests/enforcement/test_a_tool_states_its_notes_first.py", "tests/enforcement/test_clock_isolation.py", "tests/enforcement/test_public_api_has_a_caller.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-skills-under-test.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "No recorded test evidence for the reviewed commit (prawduct-hook test-status: stale, no .test-evidence.json on disk)", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The cumulative bundle (1,509 files, 1,334 commits over 74358293d) has no recorded evidence at 278bb302a, so nothing a gate reads shows the suite green at the head being merged. prawduct-hook test-status is the only freshness signal and it reads stale. Prior R-1 (rev-20261101T130955Z-6b7a9e5f) accepted the same gap for 6313c2dd1; the cited files and the whole evals tree have changed materially since, so it is raised again rather than folded into that prior. Before the develop merge, run the full gate on this head (the evals suite on the pool, ./scripts/test-integration.sh -rs with each skip accounted for) and record the evidence, or record why the accept from R-1 still covers this head. The head merge changed integration-only assertions (test_report_docs_accepted now expects 8 renders at 1200/1104/688/390 px, test_presentation_host_fit's six-arm control), which only the browser-backed integration run exercises.", "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "The pageless-batch disclosure prints an empty arm list when the set-aside runs stored no results", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. In WriterEval._run, a batch run is set aside when _stored_no_page is true, and that also holds for a run with no results at all. _aside_by_arm counts cases from each aside run's stored results and skips an arm with none. If every aside run stored nothing, `who` is empty and the disclosure reads 'A batch run that stored none, , is left out of the gated readings'. This is genuinely ambiguous, because it depends on whether a launched batch run can end with zero stored results (a lost cell normally still stores a row). If it can, fall back to the run count when `each` is empty, or assert that it is non-empty. If it cannot, no change is wanted.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/harness/writer_eval.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "GENERATOR.md's definition of done still names two render widths and a scrolling table's shadow after host pass 5 moved check.py to four widths and a fade cue", "recommendation": "Scope: class. The same commit (560fae299) updated the width list in ANALYST.md item 7, the kit README row, check.py and report_doc_checks.py, but GENERATOR.md section 8 item 1 still reads 'It renders at 1200 px and 390 px' and item 2 'at both widths'. check.py's own docstring says it automates GENERATOR section 8 items 1-3, yet it now also fails on 1104 and 688 px (the host frames that caused the pass-5 defects), on an overflowing table with no fade cue, and on a first screen at more than one measure, none of which section 8 lists. Section 7 'Layout' and 'Restraint' still describe the overflow cue as an edge shadow, where the kit now fades the frame's right edge (the shadow remains only as the fallback). A host or maintainer who uses GENERATOR section 8 as the definition of done checks two widths and passes a page that overflows in Discodon's iframe. The width list is copied into at least five places, so fixing this one copy leaves the class open. Make GENERATOR section 8 items 1-2 relational ('at every width and theme scripts/check.py renders, listed in its docstring'), and let check.py's docstring own the list and the extra checks. Change section 7's overflow-cue sentence to say what the kit does (a fade, with the shadow as the fallback) and do not restate it elsewhere. BLIND-TEST.md's '1200 and 390 px' records a past run and stays as it is.", "files": ["docs/eval-report-kit/GENERATOR.md", "docs/eval-report-kit/scripts/check.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "figure_keys.py has grown to a 7,584-line module that 32 others import, holding the key grammar, every family's resolver, the derived statistics, the grammar's teaching text and the typed-digit check", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. analysis/report/figure_keys.py is new in this changeset and is now the package's largest module: 36 public and 350 private top-level functions, plus about 140 more top-level names, imported by 32 modules. It does five separate jobs. (1) It parses keys and filters. (2) It resolves every path family: cost, price, settle, spread and compare, confusion, calls, criteria and more. (3) It derives figures: pricing a trade, the unreported-cost what-ifs, overall_interval and flip_margin, which settle.py, price.py and the scorecard use as their single source. (4) It teaches the grammar (figure_key_grammar, its index and pages). (5) It runs the D3 literal-digit check. The module already names the seams: _FAMILIES and the grammar's path groups at line 7382 sort every family into a group. Any edit to one family (a new price field, a settle variant) reopens a file that every page, analyst action and check imports, and the private helpers shared across families can only be reached from inside it. Split it along the existing groups. Keep figure_keys as the parser, the _FAMILIES registry and render. Move each group's resolvers, and the derivations they own, into a figure_keys/ subpackage, one module per group. Move the grammar's teaching text and literal_numbers into modules of their own. Keep the public names re-exported from figure_keys, so its 32 importers and the analyst's one-owner rule do not change.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/figure_keys.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "Capitalising a label has no single owner: about 18 call sites use two different idioms, and one of them (str.capitalize) lowercases the rest of the text", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Host pass 5 made 'a measure name starts with a capital where it is the label' a page rule. It implemented that rule in parts/charting.measure_words (prose=False by default, so every caller inside a sentence must pass prose=True) and again inline in parts/resolve_table.py:299. The report package already capitalises a first letter at about 18 sites. Most use name[:1].upper() + name[1:] (style.py, verdict.py, words.py, build.py, doc_page/build.py, campaign/page.py, campaign/scorecard.py, parts/tables.py). Others use str.capitalize() (doc_page/build.py:477, parts/full_results.py:412, parts/resolve_cells.py:310, parts/resolve_table.py:605, parts/words.py), which also lowercases everything after the first letter. Today's inputs to those calls (humanised dimension keys, interval levels, figure text) happen to survive that, so nothing renders wrong yet. But a maintainer who copies the nearest idiom onto an arm name, a served model or an acronym-bearing label (for example 'GPT tone' becoming 'Gpt tone') breaks the single page register this pass set out to create. Add one helper beside humanize in style.py, which upper-cases the first character only, and send every label site through it. Search the package for 'capitalize()' and '[:1].upper()' to find them all; expect members outside this diff. No edit is needed for correctness today.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/parts/charting.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/parts/resolve_table.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/parts/resolve_cells.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/parts/full_results.py", 