is/style.py:301` `_check_subject(expression)` keeps every goal-check expression a host ever names. In a long-lived host process (Discodon serving evals) both grow for the life of the process and are never released. Fix: give both a bounded `functools.lru_cache(maxsize=...)`, as the package's other read caches have. To bound the class, search `packages/evals/src` for `@cache` / `@functools.cache` on a function with a `str` (or other unbounded) parameter. Only these two were found. The zero-argument ones (`load_skill`, `_ledger`, `shipped_rubric`, `_figure_key_forms`, `package_version`, `package_rev`, `_run_started_at`) are correct as they are.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/ops/report_writer/rubric.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/style.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "WriterEval's batched, paid launch loop writes no log line: the batch launched, its reservation and the stop at the spend cap are visible only on the returned result", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `harness/writer_eval.py` (2,175 lines, new) has no logger. Its `run()` launches arms batch by batch, reserves each batch against the spend cap (around lines 1026-1080) and stops when a batch does not fit. That decision reaches the operator only as `WriterResult.launch_line` and a disclosure after `run()` returns. The engine logs each run group it starts (`run/launch.py`), so run ids and spend are recoverable, but a live run that is killed mid-way leaves nothing saying which batch it was on, what that batch reserved, or that the cap was the reason it stopped. One INFO line per batch launched (arms, cases, reservation) and one at the stop (`stopped_on`, spend), in the form `writer_pairwise.py:1081` and `definition_drift.py:996` already use, would cover it. This is a NOTE because the engine's own lines and the stored runs remain to diagnose from.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/harness/writer_eva